nodecode-eyes is a rewrite of Agenziamaia's Agenziamaia/eyes:

  https://github.com/Agenziamaia/eyes
  commit 5f30bd487be26ed9b074fddd3a4408709b92154c
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Giulio Di Stefano (agenziamaia) (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the one measurement engine, in Common Lisp against
Nodecode's own seams. The plugin's transform_tool_result hook is this cell's
:TOOL point hook, which wraps one tool dispatch and appends the notes to the
tool's own answer; ffprobe and ffmpeg are still the only things run, with the
same arguments the plugin used -- 96x96 grayscale frames for filled and
motion, ebur128 and silencedetect for loudness and silences, and an 8x1 tile
contact sheet. The three faults the plugin names (almost black or white,
nearly empty frame, barely moves, effectively silent) and the measured note's
wording are its own, so a reader of one recognises the other. The kill switch
is the section: "enabled": false, or the cell switched off in /cells.

What was left out, and why: the contact sheets go to the folder the section
names rather than into the harness's cache, because a Lisp cell has one folder
it was given and writes nowhere else; the "written in the last five minutes"
window, the three-files-a-result cap and both timeouts are section settings
here rather than constants; a tool the result does not name but the call's
arguments do is found in both, as the plugin found it by serialising the
arguments; and the plugin's $EYES_OFF environment switch is the section's own
"enabled": false, which the loader already honours. Two tools, eyes_measure
and eyes_status, and the /eyes command are additions: a model that wants to
measure a file it did not just write, and an operator who wants to see what
the cell is set to, had nothing to call in the original.
